var e=[{id:`PP03-0001`,q:`Under 14 CFR part 91, when is a 100-hour inspection required for an aircraft?`,a:[`When the aircraft is used to carry persons for hire or for flight instruction for hire.`,`Only when the aircraft is operated above 12,500 pounds.`,`On every aircraft regardless of how it is operated.`],c:0,exp:`A 100-hour inspection is required when an aircraft is used to carry any person (other than a crewmember) for hire, or is given for flight instruction for hire. Privately operated aircraft not used for hire do not require a 100-hour inspection.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0002`,q:`How often must an annual inspection be accomplished to keep an aircraft eligible to fly under 14 CFR part 91?`,a:[`Every 100 hours of time in service.`,`Within the preceding 12 calendar months.`,`Within the preceding 24 calendar months.`],c:1,exp:`An aircraft may not be operated unless it has had an annual inspection within the preceding 12 calendar months and was approved for return to service. The annual is based on calendar time, not hours.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0003`,q:`The 100-hour limit before a required 100-hour inspection may be exceeded by how much, and only for what reason?`,a:[`By 25 hours for any reason chosen by the owner.`,`By 50 hours if the engine is operating normally.`,`By 10 hours only while en route to a place where the inspection can be done.`],c:2,exp:`The 100-hour limit may be exceeded by not more than 10 hours, and only while en route to reach a place where the inspection can be performed. The excess time is then included in computing the next 100 hours of time in service.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0004`,q:`When a 100-hour inspection is exceeded by allowable time to reach an inspection facility, how is that excess time handled?`,a:[`It must be added to the next 100-hour interval, shortening it.`,`It is forgiven and the next interval starts at zero hours.`,`It is added to the next interval, extending it beyond 100 hours.`],c:0,exp:`Any time flown beyond 100 hours under the en-route allowance must be deducted from the next interval. In other words, the excess time is included when computing the next 100 hours, so the following inspection is due sooner.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0005`,q:`Who is authorized to approve an aircraft for return to service after an annual inspection?`,a:[`Any certificated airframe and powerplant (A&P) mechanic.`,`Only the holder of an inspection authorization (IA).`,`Any repairman certificate holder.`],c:1,exp:`An annual inspection must be performed and the aircraft approved for return to service by an A&P mechanic who also holds an inspection authorization (IA), or by an appropriately rated repair station or the manufacturer. An A&P without an IA may not sign off an annual.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0006`,q:`Who may perform and approve for return to service a 100-hour inspection?`,a:[`Only the holder of an inspection authorization (IA).`,`Only the engine manufacturer.`,`A certificated A&P mechanic; an inspection authorization is not required.`],c:2,exp:`A 100-hour inspection may be performed and returned to service by a certificated A&P mechanic. Unlike the annual inspection, it does not require an inspection authorization.`,ref:`14 CFR 65.85`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0007`,q:`How does the scope and detail of a 100-hour inspection compare with that of an annual inspection?`,a:[`The scope and detail are the same; the difference is who may sign it off.`,`The 100-hour is far less detailed and covers only the engine.`,`The annual covers only the airframe while the 100-hour covers only the engine.`],c:0,exp:`The 100-hour and annual inspections use the same scope and detail as listed in 14 CFR part 43 Appendix D. The practical difference is the personnel authorized to perform and approve them.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.15`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0008`,q:`Which document lists the minimum scope and detail items that must be covered during an annual or 100-hour inspection?`,a:[`14 CFR part 43 Appendix A.`,`14 CFR part 43 Appendix D.`,`14 CFR part 91 Appendix E.`],c:1,exp:`Appendix D to part 43 contains the scope and detail of items (as applicable to the particular aircraft) that must be inspected during an annual or 100-hour inspection.`,ref:`14 CFR 43 Appendix D`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0009`,q:`May an aircraft due for a 100-hour inspection be inspected and returned to service on an annual inspection instead?`,a:[`No, the two inspections are not interchangeable.`,`Only if the aircraft has flown fewer than 50 hours.`,`Yes, an annual inspection satisfies the 100-hour requirement.`],c:2,exp:`Because the annual and 100-hour have the same scope and detail, an annual inspection may be substituted for a required 100-hour inspection. The reverse is not true, since a 100-hour does not satisfy the annual requirement.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0010`,q:`Before beginning a 100-hour or annual inspection of a reciprocating engine, 14 CFR 43.15 requires the person to first`,a:[`perform a routine run-up and operational check to determine engine condition.`,`replace all life-limited engine parts.`,`remove all engine cylinders for internal inspection.`],c:0,exp:`Section 43.15 requires that, before beginning the inspection, the person run the engine (or have it run) to check items such as power output, magnetos, fuel and oil pressure, and cylinder/oil temperature, so the inspection can evaluate actual condition.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.15`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0011`,q:`For a reciprocating-engine aircraft, 14 CFR 43.15 requires that an annual or 100-hour inspection include a check of`,a:[`only the oil filter element.`,`cylinder compression and engine power output as part of determining engine condition.`,`only the exhaust system gas temperature.`],c:1,exp:`When inspecting a reciprocating engine under 43.15, the person must determine engine condition, including a check of cylinder compression and proper power output, magneto operation, and related parameters.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.15`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0012`,q:`An aircraft used only for personal, non-commercial flying has flown 130 hours since its last annual. Regarding the 100-hour inspection, the aircraft`,a:[`is grounded until a 100-hour inspection is completed.`,`must have a 100-hour inspection within the next 10 hours.`,`does not require a 100-hour inspection because it is not flown for hire.`],c:2,exp:`The 100-hour inspection requirement applies only to aircraft carrying persons for hire or used for flight instruction for hire. A privately operated aircraft needs only the annual inspection within the preceding 12 calendar months.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0013`,q:`A progressive inspection program is approved under which part 91 provision as an alternative to annual/100-hour inspections?`,a:[`14 CFR 91.409(d).`,`14 CFR 91.207.`,`14 CFR 43.3.`],c:0,exp:`Section 91.409(d) permits an owner or operator to use an approved progressive inspection program, which divides the required inspection into segments to keep the aircraft in continuous service while still covering the complete inspection.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0014`,q:`For a turbine-powered aircraft, the inspection requirements under 14 CFR 91.409 may be satisfied by`,a:[`only a 100-hour inspection performed by an A&P.`,`an inspection program selected and used in accordance with 91.409(f).`,`no inspection at all once it leaves the factory.`],c:1,exp:`Large airplanes, turbojets, turboprops, and turbine-powered rotorcraft are inspected under one of the programs in 91.409(f), such as a manufacturer's recommended program or an approved program, rather than under the basic annual/100-hour rule.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0015`,q:`"Time in service," used to compute 100-hour inspection intervals, is measured from the moment the aircraft`,a:[`engine is first started until it is shut down.`,`is pushed back from the gate until it returns to the gate.`,`leaves the surface of the earth until it touches down at the next landing.`],c:2,exp:`Time in service, with respect to maintenance records, means the time from the moment an aircraft leaves the surface of the earth until it touches down at the next point of landing. This flight time, not ground or block time, is used for the 100-hour interval.`,ref:`14 CFR 1.1`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0016`,q:`An aircraft's annual inspection was completed and signed off on March 10. The annual will next become due no later than`,a:[`the last day of March of the following year.`,`March 31 of the same year.`,`exactly 365 flight hours later.`],c:0,exp:`An annual is good for 12 calendar months, expiring on the last day of the twelfth month after it was performed. An inspection signed in March is due by the last day of March of the next year, regardless of hours flown.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0017`,q:`What is a "life-limited part" on an aircraft engine?`,a:[`A part that may be used indefinitely if it passes inspection.`,`A part that must be removed from service at a mandatory replacement time, regardless of condition.`,`A part that is replaced only when it visibly fails.`],c:1,exp:`A life-limited (hard-time) part has a mandatory retirement or replacement interval set by the manufacturer or by airworthiness limitations. It must be removed at that limit even if it appears serviceable.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0018`,q:`Mandatory replacement times for life-limited engine parts are most commonly published in the`,a:[`aircraft owner's logbook.`,`pilot's operating handbook performance section.`,`Airworthiness Limitations section of the manufacturer's ICA.`],c:2,exp:`Life limits and mandatory replacement intervals appear in the Airworthiness Limitations Section of the Instructions for Continued Airworthiness, which is FAA-approved and mandatory.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0019`,q:`The manufacturer's recommended Time Between Overhaul (TBO) for a typical part 91 piston engine is`,a:[`a recommendation, not mandatory, for many part 91 non-commercial operators.`,`a mandatory limit that may never be exceeded.`,`the same as the engine's life limit.`],c:0,exp:`For many part 91 non-commercial operations, the manufacturer's TBO is a recommendation rather than a regulatory limit. Hard-time limits become mandatory when required by an airworthiness limitation, AD, or the operating rules under which the aircraft flies.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0020`,q:`In a "hard-time" maintenance process, a component is`,a:[`left in service until a malfunction is detected.`,`overhauled or removed from service when it reaches a specified time limit.`,`inspected on condition with no fixed limit.`],c:1,exp:`Hard-time is a primary maintenance process in which an item is removed for overhaul or replacement at a specified interval, regardless of its apparent condition. It contrasts with on-condition and condition-monitoring processes.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0021`,q:`Which of the following is most likely to be a life-limited rotating part in a turbine engine?`,a:[`An access panel fastener.`,`An oil filler cap.`,`A turbine disk.`],c:2,exp:`High-energy rotating components such as turbine disks, compressor disks, and spacers are typically assigned mandatory life limits because of low-cycle fatigue. Non-structural items like caps and fasteners are not life-limited.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0022`,q:`Turbine engine disk and spool life limits are most often expressed in`,a:[`cycles, because low-cycle fatigue depends on start-stop and power cycles.`,`calendar days only.`,`gallons of fuel burned.`],c:0,exp:`Turbine rotating-component life limits are usually stated in cycles, since fatigue damage accumulates with each start-takeoff-shutdown cycle. Accurate cycle counting is essential to track remaining life.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0023`,q:`When a life-limited part reaches its published limit, the mechanic must`,a:[`re-inspect it and return it to service if no cracks are found.`,`remove and replace it even if it still appears serviceable.`,`extend the limit by 10 percent and continue using it.`],c:1,exp:`A life limit is mandatory. Once reached, the part must be removed from service and replaced; its condition at that point does not authorize continued use.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0024`,q:`To know how much life remains on a life-limited engine part, the mechanic must rely primarily on`,a:[`the engine's external appearance.`,`the color of the exhaust during run-up.`,`accurate maintenance and operational records of time/cycles since new.`],c:2,exp:`Remaining life on a life-limited part is determined from records of time in service and/or cycles accumulated since new. Without accurate records, the part may have to be retired conservatively.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0025`,q:`An engine is operated under a hard-time program. At the published interval the proper action is to`,a:[`overhaul or replace the item regardless of its measured condition.`,`test the item and reset the interval if it still works.`,`wait until the item shows wear before acting.`],c:0,exp:`Under hard-time, the item must be overhauled or replaced at the established interval. The process does not allow continued service based on a passing inspection at the limit.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0026`,q:`Which statement about TBO and life limits is correct?`,a:[`TBO and life limits always mean the same thing.`,`A recommended TBO can sometimes be exceeded, but a mandatory life limit cannot.`,`Both are merely suggestions with no regulatory force in any operation.`],c:1,exp:`A recommended TBO may be exceeded in some part 91 operations based on condition, but a mandatory life limit (from an airworthiness limitation or AD) may never be exceeded.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0027`,q:`A part's record shows it has accumulated cycles equal to its assigned life limit during the current inspection. The mechanic should`,a:[`send it for nondestructive testing and reuse it if it passes.`,`log it as airworthy with no further action.`,`scrap or retire the part so it is not reinstalled.`],c:2,exp:`A life-limited part at its limit must be retired from service. It should be removed and not reinstalled; nondestructive testing does not extend a mandatory life limit.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0028`,q:`What is a "sudden stoppage" inspection on a reciprocating engine?`,a:[`A special inspection after the engine/propeller is suddenly stopped, such as by a prop strike.`,`A scheduled overhaul performed every 100 hours.`,`A daily preflight oil-level check.`],c:0,exp:`A sudden stoppage inspection is a special inspection required after the rotating engine and propeller are abruptly stopped, for example by a propeller strike. It looks for internal damage such as a bent crankshaft and cracked gears or accessories.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0029`,q:`After a propeller strike on a reciprocating engine, a key item that must be checked is`,a:[`only the spark plug gap.`,`the crankshaft runout (and often the gear train and accessory drives) per manufacturer data.`,`only the air filter condition.`],c:1,exp:`A propeller strike can bend the crankshaft and damage the gear train, so the special inspection includes checking crankshaft runout and internal components per the manufacturer's instructions, which may require teardown.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0030`,q:`A turbine engine that has experienced an overspeed event must be`,a:[`returned to service immediately with no further checks.`,`operated at idle for one hour to clear the fault.`,`inspected per the manufacturer's overspeed inspection instructions before further flight.`],c:2,exp:`An overspeed can overstress rotating components. The manufacturer publishes special overspeed inspection requirements, which depend on the magnitude and duration of the overspeed, and these must be accomplished before return to service.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0031`,q:`Following a turbine engine over-temperature event, the depth of the required special inspection generally depends on`,a:[`the magnitude of the temperature and how long it was exceeded.`,`the color of the aircraft paint.`,`the number of passengers carried.`],c:0,exp:`Manufacturers provide over-temperature charts that relate the peak temperature and its duration to the required inspection action, ranging from a borescope inspection to hot-section removal.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0032`,q:`After an aircraft has been struck by lightning, the engine and related systems should be`,a:[`ignored, since lightning never affects engines.`,`given a special inspection for evidence of burning, pitting, or magnetism per manufacturer guidance.`,`flushed with fresh oil and immediately flown.`],c:1,exp:`A lightning strike can burn, pit, or magnetize components and damage bearings or accessories. A special inspection per the manufacturer's instructions checks for these effects before return to service.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0033`,q:`A special inspection is generally required after a hard landing primarily to check for`,a:[`paint fading on the cowling.`,`tire tread depth only.`,`damage to engine mounts, mounting structure, and related components.`],c:2,exp:`A hard landing imposes high loads that can crack or distort engine mounts and surrounding structure. The special inspection focuses on the mounts, attach points, and nearby components for cracks and deformation.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0034`,q:`Authority and detailed procedures for accomplishing a special inspection (such as sudden stoppage) come primarily from`,a:[`the manufacturer's instructions and approved data.`,`the pilot's personal preference.`,`general shop tradition with no published basis.`],c:0,exp:`Special inspections must follow the engine or airframe manufacturer's instructions and other approved data, which define what to inspect and the acceptable limits after each type of event.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0035`,q:`An engine that has ingested a significant amount of foreign material or experienced a bird strike should receive`,a:[`no inspection unless the pilot reports a problem.`,`a special inspection, often including a borescope of the gas path.`,`only a fuel system flush.`],c:1,exp:`Foreign object damage or a bird strike can nick or crack blades and other gas-path parts. A special inspection, frequently a borescope inspection, is performed to assess internal damage.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0036`,q:`After a reciprocating engine has been submerged or exposed to a flood, the recommended action is to`,a:[`run it immediately to dry it out.`,`add extra oil and continue normal flights.`,`perform a special inspection and preservation/cleaning per manufacturer instructions before any operation.`],c:2,exp:`Water can cause corrosion and contamination. The engine should be inspected, cleaned, and preserved according to the manufacturer's instructions before any attempt to operate it, to prevent internal damage.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0037`,q:`A turbine engine borescope inspection is best described as a special inspection that`,a:[`allows internal visual inspection of the gas path without major teardown.`,`requires complete engine disassembly every time.`,`can only be done with the engine running.`],c:0,exp:`A borescope lets the technician view internal areas such as compressor and turbine blades, combustor, and vanes through access ports, providing internal inspection without removing the engine or major disassembly.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0038`,q:`If a propeller strike occurs but the engine "seems to run fine," the proper course of action is to`,a:[`disregard it because the engine still runs.`,`still accomplish the required sudden-stoppage/prop-strike inspection before further flight.`,`simply replace the propeller and fly.`],c:1,exp:`Internal damage from a prop strike, such as a bent crankshaft or cracked gear, is not always evident from normal operation. The required special inspection must be completed regardless of how the engine appears to run.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0039`,q:`Which of the following is considered FAA-approved data?`,a:[`A mechanic's personal notes.`,`A magazine maintenance article.`,`A type certificate data sheet (TCDS).`],c:2,exp:`Approved data includes items such as the type certificate data sheet, airworthiness directives, manufacturer's data approved by the FAA, STCs, and FAA field approvals (Form 337). Personal notes and articles are not approved data.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0040`,q:`Which of the following is generally "acceptable data" but not by itself "approved data"?`,a:[`Advisory Circular 43.13-1B, when used as a general guide.`,`An airworthiness directive.`,`A supplemental type certificate.`],c:0,exp:`AC 43.13-1B contains methods, techniques, and practices that are acceptable to the FAA when there is no manufacturer's instruction. It becomes approved data only when an authorized person specifically approves it for a particular installation or repair.`,ref:`AC 43.13-1B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0041`,q:`A major repair to an engine generally requires`,a:[`no documentation at all.`,`approved data and is recorded on FAA Form 337.`,`only a verbal agreement with the owner.`],c:1,exp:`Major repairs and major alterations must be performed using approved data and documented on FAA Form 337. This ensures the change is based on data the FAA has accepted as airworthy.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0042`,q:`A Supplemental Type Certificate (STC) is`,a:[`acceptable data only, never approved.`,`a recommendation that may be ignored.`,`approved data for the specific alteration it covers.`],c:2,exp:`An STC is FAA-approved data authorizing a specific alteration to a type-certificated product. Its installation instructions are approved data for that change.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0043`,q:`When the manufacturer's maintenance manual gives a specific procedure and limit for an engine repair, the mechanic should`,a:[`use the manufacturer's instructions as the controlling data.`,`use any general reference and ignore the manual.`,`estimate the limits from experience.`],c:0,exp:`Manufacturer's instructions for continued airworthiness are the primary source and take precedence. General references like AC 43.13-1B are used only when there is no specific manufacturer's data.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0044`,q:`Which document is the authoritative source for an engine's specifications, limits, and approved configuration?`,a:[`The aircraft sales brochure.`,`The type certificate data sheet (TCDS).`,`A trade-association newsletter.`],c:1,exp:`The TCDS is approved data that lists the certificated specifications and limitations for the engine. It is used to verify that an engine and its components meet the approved type design.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0045`,q:`A field approval obtained on FAA Form 337 provides`,a:[`approval to ignore the airworthiness directives.`,`permission to bypass the annual inspection.`,`approved data for that specific aircraft alteration or repair.`],c:2,exp:`When an FSDO approves data on a Form 337 (a field approval), that data becomes approved data for the particular major repair or alteration on that aircraft.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0046`,q:`Manufacturer's service information becomes FAA-approved data when`,a:[`it has been specifically approved by the FAA or issued under approved procedures.`,`it is printed on company letterhead.`,`the mechanic decides it looks reasonable.`],c:0,exp:`Some manufacturer data is FAA-approved (for example, airworthiness limitations or data developed under DER/ODA authority). Letterhead alone does not make information approved; the FAA acceptance or approval status determines it.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0047`,q:`For a major repair, which of the following could NOT serve as the approved data?`,a:[`Manufacturer's FAA-approved structural repair manual.`,`An uncontrolled internet forum post.`,`Data approved through a field approval on Form 337.`],c:1,exp:`Approved data must come from a recognized, authorized source such as approved manuals, STCs, ADs, or field approvals. An uncontrolled forum post has no approval status and cannot be used.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0048`,q:`Compliance with an applicable airworthiness directive (AD) is`,a:[`optional and based on the owner's preference.`,`required only for commercial operators.`,`mandatory; the aircraft is not airworthy until the AD is complied with.`],c:2,exp:`ADs are legally enforceable rules issued to correct unsafe conditions. Compliance is mandatory for the affected product, and the aircraft is not airworthy if an applicable AD has not been complied with as required.`,ref:`14 CFR 39.7`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0049`,q:`How does a manufacturer's service bulletin (SB) differ from an airworthiness directive (AD) for a part 91 operator?`,a:[`An SB is the manufacturer's recommendation and is generally not mandatory unless made so by an AD or operating rule.`,`An SB is mandatory while an AD is advisory.`,`There is no difference between them.`],c:0,exp:`A service bulletin is the manufacturer's recommendation. For many part 91 operations it is not mandatory unless incorporated by an AD or required by the operating rules; an AD, by contrast, is always mandatory.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0050`,q:`Where are the recurring inspection and life-limit requirements that a mechanic must comply with typically published by the manufacturer?`,a:[`In the aircraft bill of sale.`,`In the Instructions for Continued Airworthiness (ICA).`,`In the pilot's logbook.`],c:1,exp:`ICA contain the manufacturer's required maintenance, inspection intervals, and airworthiness limitations needed to keep the product airworthy. Mechanics must comply with the applicable ICA.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0051`,q:`An AD specifies a one-time inspection at the next 100-hour or annual inspection. The mechanic must`,a:[`ignore the AD if the engine seems fine.`,`wait until the engine fails before complying.`,`accomplish the AD as specified and record the compliance.`],c:2,exp:`ADs must be complied with at the specified time or interval, and the compliance must be recorded in the maintenance records. The aircraft is not airworthy until the required action is done.`,ref:`14 CFR 39.7`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0052`,q:`When recording AD compliance, the maintenance record entry must include`,a:[`the AD number, method of compliance, and date, plus recurring due times if applicable.`,`only the single word complied with no other details.`,`nothing, because AD compliance is not recorded.`],c:0,exp:`An AD compliance entry should identify the AD number, the method of compliance, and the date, and for recurring ADs note when the next action is due. This makes the airworthiness status traceable.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.417`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0053`,q:`A service letter from an engine manufacturer is generally`,a:[`a mandatory regulatory requirement equal to an AD.`,`informational or recommended guidance, not mandatory unless made so by another rule.`,`an order from the FAA.`],c:1,exp:`Service letters typically provide product information, recommendations, or clarifications. They are not mandatory for part 91 operators unless adopted by an AD or required by the operating rules.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0054`,q:`An AD applies to an engine model installed on an aircraft. The AD applicability is determined by`,a:[`the color of the aircraft.`,`the age of the pilot.`,`the make and model/part affected, as stated in the AD's applicability section.`],c:2,exp:`Each AD has an applicability statement identifying the affected products by make, model, and sometimes serial number or part number. The mechanic compares the aircraft/engine to that applicability to determine if the AD applies.`,ref:`14 CFR 39.7`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0055`,q:`Mandatory portions of the Instructions for Continued Airworthiness, such as the Airworthiness Limitations Section, are`,a:[`FAA-approved and must be complied with.`,`optional suggestions for all operators.`,`applicable only to experimental aircraft.`],c:0,exp:`The Airworthiness Limitations Section of the ICA is FAA-approved and contains mandatory replacement times and required inspections. Compliance is required for continued airworthiness.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0056`,q:`A recurring AD requires inspection every 50 hours. After complying, the mechanic should record`,a:[`only that the engine ran.`,`the compliance and the next due time so the recurring action is tracked.`,`nothing until the AD is superseded.`],c:1,exp:`For recurring ADs, the record must show compliance and the time/date the next inspection is due. This allows future maintenance to keep the recurring requirement current.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.417`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0057`,q:`If a service bulletin has been made mandatory by an AD, the operator must`,a:[`treat it as optional.`,`comply only if convenient.`,`comply with it as required by the AD.`],c:2,exp:`When an AD references and requires a service bulletin, that bulletin's actions become mandatory through the AD. The operator must comply within the AD's specified terms.`,ref:`14 CFR 39.7`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0058`,q:`Under 14 CFR 43.9, a maintenance record entry for work performed (other than an inspection) must include`,a:[`a description of the work, the date completed, and the signature and certificate number of the person approving the work.`,`only the technician's first name.`,`the cost of the parts only.`],c:0,exp:`Section 43.9 requires a description of the work performed, the date of completion, the name of the person who did the work if other than the signer, and the signature and certificate number of the person approving the aircraft for return to service.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.9`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0059`,q:`Which regulation prescribes the content of the record entry made after an inspection (such as an annual or 100-hour)?`,a:[`14 CFR 43.3.`,`14 CFR 43.11.`,`14 CFR 91.151.`],c:1,exp:`Section 43.11 prescribes the content of records for inspections performed under parts 91, 125, and 135, including the type of inspection, date, time in service, and the required statement and signature.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.11`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0060`,q:`After completing an annual inspection in which the aircraft is found airworthy, the entry must include the statement`,a:[`an informal note that the inspection looked fine to the mechanic.`,`no statement of any kind regarding the inspection result.`,`a certification that the aircraft was inspected in accordance with an annual inspection and determined to be in airworthy condition.`],c:2,exp:`Section 43.11 specifies the exact certifying language for an airworthy inspection result, including the type and scope of inspection and the determination of airworthy condition, along with the signature and certificate information.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.11`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0061`,q:`If an annual inspection reveals the aircraft is NOT airworthy, the person performing it must`,a:[`provide the owner a signed and dated list of discrepancies.`,`destroy the maintenance records.`,`approve the aircraft for return to service anyway.`],c:0,exp:`Under 43.11, if the aircraft is found unairworthy, the inspector must give the owner or lessee a signed and dated list of the discrepancies and unairworthy items rather than approving it for return to service.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.11`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0062`,q:`How long must records of maintenance, preventive maintenance, and alterations be retained under 14 CFR 91.417?`,a:[`For exactly 30 days.`,`Until the work is repeated, superseded, or for one year after the work was performed.`,`Forever, with no exceptions for any record.`],c:1,exp:`Records of maintenance, preventive maintenance, and alterations must be retained until the work is repeated, superseded by other work, or for one year after the work is performed, whichever comes first. Certain records (total time, status of ADs, etc.) must be retained and transferred with the aircraft.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.417`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0063`,q:`Which of the following records must be kept and transferred with the aircraft when it is sold (per 14 CFR 91.417)?`,a:[`The owner's fuel receipts.`,`The pilot's medical certificate.`,`The current status of applicable airworthiness directives.`],c:2,exp:`The regulation requires that records such as total time in service, current AD status, and current status of life-limited parts be retained and transferred with the aircraft at sale.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.417`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0064`,q:`An A&P mechanic performs and approves a 100-hour inspection. The record entry must include the mechanic's`,a:[`signature and certificate number.`,`home address and phone number.`,`date of birth.`],c:0,exp:`Maintenance and inspection record entries require the signature, certificate number, and kind of certificate held by the person approving the work. Personal details like address or birth date are not required.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.11`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0065`,q:`Who is primarily responsible for keeping the aircraft's maintenance records under part 91?`,a:[`The nearest repair station.`,`The owner or operator of the aircraft.`,`The fuel supplier.`],c:1,exp:`Section 91.417 places responsibility for keeping required maintenance records on the owner or operator. Mechanics make the entries, but the owner/operator must retain the records.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.417`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0066`,q:`A record entry for an inspection must include the aircraft total time in service because it`,a:[`is needed to calculate the sales tax.`,`determines the paint scheme.`,`establishes the basis for the inspection interval and continued airworthiness tracking.`],c:2,exp:`Time in service at the inspection is required by 43.11 and is used to track inspection intervals, life-limited parts, and AD compliance. It is essential continued-airworthiness information.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.11`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0067`,q:`When a mechanic returns an engine to service after a repair, the entry approving return to service is required by`,a:[`14 CFR 43.9.`,`14 CFR 21.197.`,`14 CFR 61.51.`],c:0,exp:`Section 43.9 governs the content of the record entry for maintenance, preventive maintenance, and alterations (other than 43.11 inspections), including the approval for return to service.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.9`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0068`,q:`Records that must be kept include the current status of life-limited parts. This is so the operator can`,a:[`calculate hangar rent.`,`determine remaining life and replace parts before their limits.`,`set the cabin temperature.`],c:1,exp:`Tracking the current status (time/cycles) of life-limited parts ensures they are retired at the correct limit. Section 91.417 requires this record be maintained and transferred with the aircraft.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.417`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0069`,q:`During a differential compression check, low compression accompanied by air escaping from the exhaust pipe usually indicates`,a:[`leaking intake valves.`,`worn or stuck piston rings.`,`leaking exhaust valves.`],c:2,exp:`Air heard escaping at the exhaust pipe indicates the exhaust valve is leaking and not sealing. Air at the carburetor/intake indicates an intake valve leak, and air at the crankcase breather indicates ring or cylinder wear.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0070`,q:`During a differential compression check, audible air leakage at the crankcase breather or oil filler indicates`,a:[`worn piston rings or cylinder walls.`,`a leaking exhaust valve.`,`a cracked intake manifold.`],c:0,exp:`Air escaping past the rings into the crankcase, heard at the breather or oil filler, indicates worn rings or cylinder walls. Exhaust or intake valve leaks are heard at the tailpipe or induction system instead.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0071`,q:`Magnetic particle inspection is used to detect`,a:[`cracks in aluminum and other nonmagnetic parts.`,`surface and slightly subsurface cracks in ferromagnetic parts.`,`internal voids deep within nonmetallic composites.`],c:1,exp:`Magnetic particle inspection detects surface and near-surface discontinuities, but only in ferromagnetic (magnetizable) materials such as steel crankshafts and gears. Nonmagnetic materials require other methods.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0072`,q:`Dye penetrant inspection is suitable for detecting`,a:[`subsurface cracks in steel that do not reach the surface.`,`internal corrosion not visible at the surface.`,`surface cracks open to the surface in nonporous metals, including aluminum.`],c:2,exp:`Dye penetrant reveals discontinuities that are open to the surface in nonporous materials, and works on both magnetic and nonmagnetic metals. It cannot detect subsurface flaws that do not reach the surface.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0073`,q:`After performing a magnetic particle inspection on a steel engine part, the part must be`,a:[`demagnetized before being returned to service.`,`left magnetized to improve wear resistance.`,`heated to red hot to relieve stress.`],c:0,exp:`Residual magnetism can attract ferrous debris and interfere with instruments, so parts must be demagnetized after magnetic particle inspection before being returned to service.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0074`,q:`When inspecting engine cylinders for cracks, special attention is given to areas such as`,a:[`the propeller spinner only.`,`spark plug bosses, valve seats, and cooling fins.`,`the cockpit instrument panel.`],c:1,exp:`Cylinder cracks commonly form at high-stress, high-temperature areas such as spark plug and injector bosses, around valve seats, and at the base of cooling fins. These areas warrant close inspection.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0075`,q:`When inspecting an exhaust system, a primary concern is`,a:[`the color of the exhaust paint.`,`the brand name stamped on the muffler.`,`cracks and leaks that could cause a fire or carbon monoxide hazard.`],c:2,exp:`Exhaust system cracks and leaks can start fires and allow carbon monoxide into the cabin through the heat exchanger. Inspecting for cracks, leaks, and security is a critical safety task.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0076`,q:`Before performing a differential compression check, the cylinder should be`,a:[`at normal operating temperature.`,`completely cold to get the best reading.`,`filled with solvent.`],c:0,exp:`The differential compression test is performed with the engine warm (at operating temperature) so that parts have expanded to their normal running clearances, giving representative readings.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0077`,q:`During engine inspection, oil filter or screen examination is performed to`,a:[`measure the octane of the fuel.`,`look for metal particles that may indicate internal wear or failure.`,`check the tire pressure.`],c:1,exp:`Cutting open the oil filter or inspecting the screen reveals metal particles that can signal abnormal internal wear, bearing failure, or other distress, allowing problems to be caught early.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0078`,q:`When servicing spark plugs during inspection, normal wear and serviceability are judged by`,a:[`the plug's resale value.`,`the color of the ceramic insulator box.`,`electrode wear, gap, and deposits compared with manufacturer limits.`],c:2,exp:`Spark plugs are inspected for electrode erosion, correct gap, and deposit/lead fouling, and compared against the manufacturer's serviceable limits. Plugs outside limits are cleaned, regapped, or replaced.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0079`,q:`A turbine engine hot-section inspection commonly checks combustion liners and turbine blades for`,a:[`cracks, burning, warping, and erosion.`,`correct paint gloss.`,`serial-number font size.`],c:0,exp:`Hot-section inspection looks for thermal distress such as cracks, burning, warping, erosion, and coating loss on combustion liners, nozzles, and turbine blades, comparing against service limits.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0080`,q:`If a differential compression reading is below the manufacturer's limit, an accepted next step is to`,a:[`immediately scrap the entire engine.`,`"stake" or run the engine and recheck, then investigate per manufacturer guidance.`,`ignore it and return the aircraft to service.`],c:1,exp:`A low reading may be caused by a valve not fully seated. The accepted practice is to run the engine and recheck, and if still low, investigate the cause (valves, rings, cylinder) following manufacturer procedures before deciding on cylinder removal.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0081`,q:`When inspecting a reciprocating engine ignition harness, the technician checks for`,a:[`the manufacturer's marketing logo.`,`the resale value of the wires.`,`chafing, cracked insulation, and security of the leads and connectors.`],c:2,exp:`Ignition harness inspection covers insulation cracking, chafing, corrosion at connectors, and secure routing, since faults here cause misfiring and electrical leakage.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0082`,q:`A common purpose of an engine mount in a typical light aircraft installation is to`,a:[`support the engine and transmit its loads to the airframe while helping isolate vibration.`,`store engine oil.`,`act as the primary fuel tank.`],c:0,exp:`The engine mount supports the engine and transmits thrust and other loads to the airframe structure, while shock-mount (vibration isolator) units help dampen and isolate engine vibration from the airframe.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0083`,q:`A Dynafocal engine mount is designed so that the shock mounts are`,a:[`mounted vertically only.`,`angled so their axes point toward the engine's center of gravity.`,`eliminated entirely from the installation.`],c:1,exp:`In a Dynafocal mount, the vibration isolators are angled so their centerlines intersect at the engine's center of gravity. This arrangement improves vibration isolation compared with earlier conical (bed) mounts.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0084`,q:`Lord mounts (engine shock mounts) are inspected during engine inspection for`,a:[`correct radio frequency.`,`fuel octane rating.`,`deterioration, cracking, separation, and oil soaking of the rubber.`],c:2,exp:`Rubber vibration isolators (Lord mounts) degrade from age, heat, and oil exposure. They are checked for cracking, separation from the metal, swelling, and oil soaking, and replaced when deteriorated.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0085`,q:`A welded tubular steel engine mount should be inspected primarily for`,a:[`cracks, corrosion, and distortion, especially at welds and attach fittings.`,`the brand of paint used.`,`the type of upholstery installed.`],c:0,exp:`Tubular steel mounts are inspected for cracks (often originating at welds), corrosion, dents, and bent or distorted members, as well as security and condition of attach fittings.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0086`,q:`When inspecting engine mount bolts and attaching hardware, the technician should verify`,a:[`only that the bolts are shiny.`,`correct torque, proper safetying, and no cracks, corrosion, or elongated holes.`,`that the bolts match the seat covers.`],c:1,exp:`Mounting hardware must be the correct part, properly torqued and safetied, and free of cracks, corrosion, and wear. Elongated bolt holes or loose hardware indicate movement and require correction.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0087`,q:`If a crack is suspected in a steel tube engine mount but is not clearly visible, an appropriate inspection method is`,a:[`guessing based on engine sound.`,`repainting the mount to hide it.`,`dye penetrant or magnetic particle inspection per approved procedures.`],c:2,exp:`Suspected cracks in mount structure are confirmed using nondestructive inspection such as dye penetrant or, for ferrous material, magnetic particle inspection, performed in accordance with approved data.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0088`,q:`Deteriorated rubber vibration isolators in an engine mount can lead to`,a:[`excessive engine movement and increased vibration transmitted to the airframe.`,`improved fuel economy.`,`higher oil pressure.`],c:0,exp:`When the rubber isolators are worn, cracked, or oil-soaked, the engine can move excessively and transmit more vibration to the airframe, which can damage structure, accessories, and lines. They must be replaced as required.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0089`,q:`After a hard landing, the engine mount should be inspected because`,a:[`the mount changes color after hard landings.`,`the high impact loads can crack or distort the mount and its attach points.`,`hard landings only affect the avionics.`],c:1,exp:`Hard-landing loads pass through the engine mount and can crack welds, bend tubes, or distort attach fittings. A special inspection of the mount and surrounding structure is warranted.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0090`,q:`When replacing engine shock mounts, the technician must use`,a:[`any rubber bushing of similar size.`,`wooden blocks as a substitute.`,`the part specified by the manufacturer's approved data for that installation.`],c:2,exp:`Shock mounts must be the correct, approved part for the specific installation, since their stiffness and geometry are matched to the engine and airframe. Substituting non-approved parts is not permitted.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0091`,q:`During an annual inspection of a reciprocating engine, the technician finds carbon and lead deposits on several spark plugs. The correct action is to`,a:[`clean, gap, and test the plugs, replacing any beyond serviceable limits.`,`discard all plugs regardless of condition.`,`reinstall them without cleaning.`],c:0,exp:`Spark plugs with deposits are cleaned, regapped, and tested. Those within serviceable limits are reinstalled (often rotated), while plugs worn beyond limits are replaced per manufacturer guidance.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0092`,q:`An aircraft is operated for hire and has flown 98 hours since its last 100-hour inspection. Regarding continued operation for hire, the operator`,a:[`may fly another 200 hours before any inspection.`,`must have a 100-hour inspection done before exceeding 100 hours (plus only the en-route allowance).`,`needs only a preflight check.`],c:1,exp:`For an aircraft used for hire, a 100-hour inspection is required each 100 hours of time in service, exceedable only by up to 10 hours and only to reach an inspection facility. The operator must arrange the inspection before exceeding the limit.`,ref:`14 CFR 91.409`,acs:`AM.III.C.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0093`,q:`Which best describes the difference between "approved" and "acceptable" data?`,a:[`They are identical terms.`,`Acceptable data outranks approved data.`,`Approved data has been formally accepted by the FAA for a use; acceptable data is good practice but is not by itself FAA-approved.`],c:2,exp:`Approved data (TCDS, ADs, STCs, FAA-approved manuals, field approvals) has FAA approval for the intended use. Acceptable data (such as AC 43.13-1B) describes accepted methods but is not approved unless an authorized person approves it for the specific job.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0094`,q:`A turbine engine experiences an in-flight overspeed of the high-pressure rotor. Before the next flight, the operator must`,a:[`accomplish the manufacturer's overspeed special inspection appropriate to the event.`,`do nothing because turbine rotors cannot overspeed.`,`simply log the event and continue flying.`],c:0,exp:`Overspeed events overstress rotating components. The manufacturer's overspeed inspection instructions, scaled to the magnitude and duration, must be accomplished before the engine is returned to service.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0095`,q:`Where would a mechanic look to find the FAA-approved mandatory replacement time for a specific turbine engine disk?`,a:[`The aircraft's weight and balance sheet.`,`The Airworthiness Limitations Section of the manufacturer's ICA (and any applicable AD).`,`A general aviation magazine.`],c:1,exp:`Mandatory life limits for parts such as turbine disks are published in the FAA-approved Airworthiness Limitations Section of the ICA, and may also be established or revised by an applicable AD.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0096`,q:`An entry approving an aircraft for return to service after maintenance constitutes`,a:[`a guarantee of resale value.`,`a transfer of aircraft ownership.`,`a certification that the work was done in accordance with the applicable requirements.`],c:2,exp:`Signing the approval for return to service certifies that the maintenance was performed in accordance with applicable standards and approved/acceptable data, and that the item is airworthy for that work.`,ref:`14 CFR 43.9`,acs:`AM.III.C.K6`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0097`,q:`When inspecting engine controls (throttle, mixture, propeller) during an inspection, the technician verifies`,a:[`full and free travel, correct rigging, and security with no binding.`,`the color of the control knobs.`,`the manufacturer's stock price.`],c:0,exp:`Engine control inspection confirms the controls move freely through full travel, reach their stops, are correctly rigged, and are secure without binding, since improper rigging affects power and safety.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0098`,q:`An AD requires repetitive inspections "unless previously accomplished." This means the operator`,a:[`never has to do the inspection.`,`may credit prior compliance that meets the AD, then continue the required recurring actions.`,`must replace the engine immediately.`],c:1,exp:`Such wording allows the operator to take credit for inspections already done that satisfy the AD, while still continuing any recurring inspections the AD requires going forward.`,ref:`14 CFR 39.7`,acs:`AM.III.C.K5`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0099`,q:`Which inspection method would be most appropriate to find a surface crack in an aluminum accessory housing?`,a:[`Magnetic particle inspection.`,`No inspection is possible on aluminum.`,`Dye penetrant inspection.`],c:2,exp:`Aluminum is nonmagnetic, so magnetic particle inspection cannot be used. Dye penetrant inspection detects surface-breaking cracks in nonferrous metals such as aluminum castings and housings.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K7`,diagram:null},{id:`PP03-0100`,q:`During engine mount inspection, an elongated (egg-shaped) bolt hole at an attach fitting most likely indicates`,a:[`relative movement and wear that must be corrected before return to service.`,`normal manufacturing tolerance requiring no action.`,`an improvement that increases strength.`],c:0,exp:`An elongated bolt hole shows that the joint has been moving, which reduces clamp-up and can lead to failure. The cause must be corrected and the affected hardware/structure repaired or replaced per approved data.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.C.K8`,diagram:null}];export{e as questions};